<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heijō-kyō</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heijō-kyō was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from 710 to 784, on the site of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a>. Built on a grid plan modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, it gave its name to the Nara period, when the Buddhist temples <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todai-ji">Tōdai-ji</a> and Kōfuku-ji were built and the ritsuryō state reached its height. Its palace site is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heijo-kyo.jpg" alt="Heijō-kyō" width="320" height="273" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wikiwikiyarou · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heijokyo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Heijō-kyō, also called Heizei-kyō and sometimes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> no miyako, was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> during most of the Nara period, from 710 to 740 and again from 745 to 784. Empress Genmei ordered the imperial capital moved from Fujiwara-kyō in 708, and the move was complete in 710. The city was modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, the capital of Tang-dynasty China, though it lacked walls.</p><p>Merchants and traders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> introduced various foreign cultures to the city through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>, and it flourished as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s first international and political capital. Its population peaked at between 50,000 and 100,000. The city formed an irregular rectangle and covered more than 25 square kilometres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The central street of the capital, the Suzaku-ōji, ran 3. 7 kilometres from north to south, from the Rajōmon Gate, the main entrance to the city in the south, to the Suzakumon Gate, the main gate on the south side of the palace complex. It was designed as a road and also to receive foreign envoys, and its size and the streetscape around it were meant to be spectacular. Excavations since 1974 have traced its route.</p><p>They show a width of about 74 metres between the centres of the side gutters, and about 85 metres between the east and west embankments at Sanjō. Below the road of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> period lay the remains of an earlier road 23 metres wide. A 220-metre stretch was designated a National Historic Site in 1984, and in 2010 a part of the avenue, 70 metres wide and 210 metres long, was restored south of the Suzakumon Gate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site lies in the modern city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a>. The imperial palace is listed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> together with other places in Nara, under the name Historic Monuments of Ancient Nara. In the area of the old capital stand ancient Buddhist temples, some of them also listed: Daian-ji, Gangō-ji, Kōfuku-ji, Saidai-ji, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todai-ji">Tōdai-ji</a> and Yakushi-ji. The Daigokuden and the Suzakumon have been reconstructed. The year 2010 marked 1,300 years since the establishment of the capital, and events with displays of national treasures and cultural properties were held in and around Nara Prefecture from 24 April to 7 November 2010.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Heijō-kyō” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 710 the court moved to the planned capital of <b>Heijo-kyo</b> at Nara, and the first histories, the Kojiki of 712 and the Nihon shoki of 720, presented the emperors as descendants of the sun goddess.</blockquote><a href="/state/ritsuryo-japan">Ritsuryo Japan</a></li></ul></section>
